{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fb697056-7f0f-4ddc-96b8-663106446b0a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Protein-ligand interactions"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e656dbe2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## **Aim of this notebook**\n",
    "\n",
    "In this notebook, we focus on **protein-ligand interactions**. Understanding such interactions, which are driving **molecular recognition**, is **fundamental** in **drug design**.\n",
    "\n",
    "To this end, we use **two Python tools**: the first one, called the **Protein–Ligand Interaction Profiler (PLIP)**, to gain insight into **protein-ligand interactions** for any **sample complex**, and the second, **py3Dmol**, to **visualize the interactions in 3D**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bee87c17",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## I. Theory"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f4f49948",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### **I.1 Protein-ligand interactions**\n",
    "\n",
    "**Ligand binding** is mainly governed by **non-covalent interactions** between the **ligand** and the surface of the **protein pocket** or the **protein-protein interface**. This process is a function of **electrostatic and shape complementarities**, **induced fitting**, **desolvation processes**, and more.\n",
    "\n",
    "Some quotes from the literature:\n",
    "\n",
    "Adapted from **[José L. Medina-Franco, Oscar Méndez-Lucio, Karina Martinez-Mayorga](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1876162314000029)**:\n",
    "\n",
    "> Understanding **protein–ligand interactions (PLIs)** and **protein–protein interactions (PPIs)** is at the core of **molecular recognition** and has a **fundamental role** in many **scientific areas**. **PLIs** and **PPIs** have a broad range of **practical applications** in **drug discovery**, including but not limited to **molecular docking**, **structure-based design**, **virtual screening** of **molecular fragments**, **small molecules**, and other types of compounds, **clustering of complexes**, and **structural interpretation of activity cliffs**, to name a few."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dc565beb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "These interactions can be rationalized in several ways, which for example opens the door to systematic analysis of the docking solutions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4e22a123-85dd-46e4-a992-97df8b9f87c4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "![protein ligand non-covalent interactions](https://projects.volkamerlab.org/teachopencadd/_images/protein_ligand_non_covalent.gif)\n",
    "\n",
    "_Figure 1_ : **Frequency of non-colavent interactions.** Out of 750,873 ligand–protein atom pairs extracted from complexes from the PDB data base, the 100 most frequent pairs can be grouped into seven interaction types, as shown in the figure (taken from the paper by de Freitas and Schapira, [A systematic analysis of atomic protein–ligand interactions in the PDB](https://doi.org/10.1039/C7MD00381A), 2017)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "016314c3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There are several programs to assess protein-ligand interactions in an automated way.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, the Kinase-Ligand Interaction Fingerprints and Structures database ([KLIFS](https://klifs.net/)) is a kinase centric tool with a freely available web service. More details can be found in **notebook T014**: \"Data acquisition from KLIFS\"."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b3e7afb5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### I.2 PLIP: Protein–Ligand Interaction Profiler"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2d5ccb00",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For a more general set of proteins, [PLIP](https://plip.biotec.tu-dresden.de/plip-web/plip/index) is also popular thanks to its publicly available webserver and free-to-use Python library."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8bd5a4f0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### I.2.1 PLIP: web service\n",
    "\n",
    "The PLIP web service is a freely available tool which allows to exhibit protein-ligand interactions from any PDB structure as shown in Figure 2 below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d7a7d53e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "![aromatic interaction](https://projects.volkamerlab.org/teachopencadd/_images/3POZ_PLIP.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "_Figure 2_ : **Visualization of protein-ligand interactions generated by the PLIP web service.** The example displays an EGFR kinase complex, associated PDB ID [3POZ](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/3POZ). The figure, a snapshot from the [web service](https://projects.biotec.tu-dresden.de/plip-web/plip) result, shows\n",
    "\n",
    "- the 3D plot of the protein and the ligand and the different interactions types detected (see legend on the right).\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- an example table describing the hydrophobic interactions between atoms from the protein and the ligand."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "209d8846",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### **I.2.2 PLIP: Algorithm**\n",
    "\n",
    "For each **binding site**, the **PLIP algorithm** considers the atoms from the **protein** and the **ligand** only if they lie within a certain **distance cutoff**. Once the **protein** and **ligand atoms** that potentially interact are identified, **non-covalent interactions** can be detected, such as:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Hydrophobic interactions**\n",
    "- **Hydrogen bonds**\n",
    "- **Aromatic stacking**\n",
    "- **Pi-cation interactions**\n",
    "- **Salt bridges**\n",
    "- **Water-bridged hydrogen bonds**\n",
    "- **Halogen bonds**\n",
    "\n",
    "These interactions are defined using **geometric rules**, such as **distance** and **angle thresholds**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7ec86b4e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The [supporting information](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4489249/bin/supp_gkv315_nar-00254-web-b-2015-File003.pdf) accompanying the manuscript ([_Nucl. Acids Res._ (2015), __43__, W1, W443-W447](https://academic.oup.com/nar/article/43/W1/W443/2467865)) describes the binding site detection and protein-ligand interactions in detail, please see the PDF document below. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "01746e73",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### **Visualization: complex and interactions**\n",
    "\n",
    "We will use **`py3Dmol`** for **visualization**. It's a **web-based molecular viewer** that can be run on **Jupyter notebooks**. We will first use it in a **basic way** to visualize a **complex of interest**. Then, we will visualize **protein-ligand interactions**.\n",
    "\n",
    "More details can be found in **notebook T019** on **advanced py3Dmol usage**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9189623e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## II.Practical"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "de5d94d7",
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": true,
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Import libraries\n",
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "import time\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")\n",
    "\n",
    "import requests\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import py3Dmol \n",
    "import openbabel\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from matplotlib import colors\n",
    "from plip.structure.preparation import PDBComplex\n",
    "from plip.exchange.report import BindingSiteReport"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "d7ccf19f",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Absolute path\n",
    "HERE = Path(_dh[-1])\n",
    "DATA = HERE / \"data\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "45e1dd20",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### II.1 PDB complex: example with EGFR"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ef31b2ea",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As a test case for this notebook, we choose the EGFR kinase. The considered PDB structure is given by the ID [3POZ](http://dx.doi.org/10.2210/pdb3poz/pdb). Let's use `py3Dmol` to visualize the structure in a notebook cell.\n",
    "\n",
    "_Note_: the complex can easily be changed by adapting the PDB ID in the cell below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "0bd3b419",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "pdb_id = \"3poz\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "e346e6d9-2ca2-466b-8e0a-0ba5b1e7f20d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "442179"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 4,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "pdb_id = pdb_id.upper()\n",
    "url = f\"https://files.rcsb.org/download/{pdb_id}.pdb\"\n",
    "response = requests.get(url)\n",
    "response.raise_for_status()  # raises if pdb_id doesn't exist or download fails\n",
    "\n",
    "# write to file for later use\n",
    "pdb_path = DATA / f\"{pdb_id}.pdb\"\n",
    "pdb_path.write_bytes(response.content)"
   ]
  },
